510 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/P 103 0 R/T(Complexity: all three are O\(1\) time and O\(1\) space per entry. round\(\) is the right choice because it recovers the intended whole number from a value that is only close to it.)/E(Complexity: all three are O\(1\) time and O\(1\) space per entry. round\(\) is the right choice because it recovers the intended whole number from a value that is only close to it.)/Pg 16 0 R/K[64]>>
endobj

511 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/P 103 0 R/T(Precedence \(high to low\): ** , unary + -, * / // %, binary + -, comparisons, not, and, or. Operators of the same level group left to right \(left-associative\), but ** is right-associative: 2**3**2 = 2**\(3**2\) = 512. Unary minus vs **: ** binds tighter than unary minus on its left, so -2**2 = -\(2**2\) = -4, while \(-2\)**2 = 4. Comparison chaining: 2.0 <= fat <= 7.0 means \(2.0 <= fat\) and \(fat <= 7.0\), with fat evaluated once. Boolean operators and / or / not combine conditions and short-circuit.)/E(Precedence \(high to low\): ** , unary + -, * / // %, binary + -, comparisons, not, and, or. Operators of the same level group left to right \(left-associative\), but ** is right-associative: 2**3**2 = 2**\(3**2\) = 512. Unary minus vs **: ** binds tighter than unary minus on its left, so -2**2 = -\(2**2\) = -4, while \(-2\)**2 = 4. Comparison chaining: 2.0 <= fat <= 7.0 means \(2.0 <= fat\) and \(fat <= 7.0\), with fat evaluated once. Boolean operators and / or / not combine conditions and short-circuit.)/Pg 16 0 R/K[65]>>
endobj

512 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/P 103 0 R/T(Three unexpected results observed: \(1\) \(4.2-3.5\)/0.1 = 7.000000000000002; \(2\) \(3.1-3.5\)/0.1 = -3.999999999999999; \(3\) \(8.7-8.5\)/0.1 = 1.999999999999993. \(The exact tail depends on the numbers; the result is near, not equal to, the integer.\) A fourth surprise: round\(-2.5\) = -2 because Python rounds halves to the even number.)/E(Three unexpected results observed: \(1\) \(4.2-3.5\)/0.1 = 7.000000000000002; \(2\) \(3.1-3.5\)/0.1 = -3.999999999999999; \(3\) \(8.7-8.5\)/0.1 = 1.999999999999993. \(The exact tail depends on the numbers; the result is near, not equal to, the integer.\) A fourth surprise: round\(-2.5\) = -2 because Python rounds halves to the even number.)/Pg 16 0 R/K[66]>>
endobj

513 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/P 103 0 R/T(round\(\), int\(\), math.floor\(\) for negatives: round\(-2.5\) = -2 \(nearest, half to even\); int\(-2.5\) = -2 \(truncates toward zero\); floor\(-2.5\) = -3 \(toward minus infinity\).)/E(round\(\), int\(\), math.floor\(\) for negatives: round\(-2.5\) = -2 \(nearest, half to even\); int\(-2.5\) = -2 \(truncates toward zero\); floor\(-2.5\) = -3 \(toward minus infinity\).)/Pg 16 0 R/K[67]>>
endobj

