endobj

1021 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/P 113 0 R/T(Yes, with a streaming design. About 20 entries per farmer per ten days gives roughly 3 million entries. From Task 10, calculating 150,000 rates took about 0.1-0.2 s, so 3 million entries is about 2-4 s of calculation, which is acceptable for a payment run every ten days. Storing every entry as Decimal objects would need on the order of a gigabyte, so the program should read entries from a file and keep only a running total per farmer \(O\(1\) memory per farmer, O\(n\) time\); the rate table keeps the rate calculation cheap. For the block level \(6,000 farmers, 120,000 entries\) memory is not an issue at all.)/E(Yes, with a streaming design. About 20 entries per farmer per ten days gives roughly 3 million entries. From Task 10, calculating 150,000 rates took about 0.1-0.2 s, so 3 million entries is about 2-4 s of calculation, which is acceptable for a payment run every ten days. Storing every entry as Decimal objects would need on the order of a gigabyte, so the program should read entries from a file and keep only a running total per farmer \(O\(1\) memory per farmer, O\(n\) time\); the rate table keeps the rate calculation cheap. For the block level \(6,000 farmers, 120,000 entries\) memory is not an issue at all.)/Pg 26 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

